 ### [SDF-based Ambient Occlusion](1881-sdf-based-ambient-occlusion.md)
-Estimates occlusion by sampling an SDF along the surface normal at increasing distances, weighting closer samples more heavily. It uses the distance field to simulate light blockage based on object proximity, avoiding the unrealistic results of SSAO which relies on depth buffers. Unlike SSAO, this method leverages global geometric information stored in the SDF, providing more accurate occlusion in scenes with complex geometry or self-occlusion.
+Estimates occlusion by sampling an SDF along the surface normal at increasing distances, weighting closer samples more heavily. It uses the distance field to simulate light blockage based on object proximity, avoiding the unrealistic results of SSAO which relies on depth buffers. Unlike SSAO, this method leverages global geometric information stored in the SDF, providing more accurate occlusion in scenes with complex geometry or self-occlusion. Choose this when global geometric accuracy is required and an SDF is available.
 
 ### [GVDB Ambient Occlusion](2193-gvdb-ambient-occlusion.md)
-Estimates visibility by querying a hierarchical voxel grid (GVDB volume) using either point sampling or ray casting. It distinguishes itself by operating directly on a voxel grid, supporting discrete point queries for speed and continuous ray casting for smoother gradients, both utilizing a tangent-space basis for rotation.
+Estimates visibility by querying a hierarchical voxel grid (GVDB volume) using either point sampling or ray casting. It distinguishes itself by operating directly on a voxel grid, supporting discrete point queries for speed and continuous ray casting for smoother gradients, both utilizing a tangent-space basis for rotation. Choose this for dynamic voxel-based scenes requiring device-side computation.
 
 ### [Screen-Space Ambient Occlusion (SSAO)](3980-screen-space-ambient-occlusion-ssao.md)
-A post-processing technique that estimates ambient occlusion by sampling points in a hemisphere around each pixel's surface position and checking if those points are occluded by geometry in the depth buffer. It is the first screen-space method, using only the Z-Buffer, making its cost independent of scene complexity and dependent only on output resolution.
+A post-processing technique that estimates ambient occlusion by sampling points in a hemisphere around each pixel's surface position and checking if those points are occluded by geometry in the depth buffer. It is the first screen-space method, using only the Z-Buffer, making its cost independent of scene complexity and dependent only on output resolution. Choose this for real-time rendering where scene geometry is not directly accessible.
 
 ### [Disk-Based Ambient Occlusion](4333-disk-based-ambient-occlusion.md)
-Approximates surface geometry as a collection of disk-shaped elements at mesh vertices to compute AO and bent normals analytically, avoiding the cost of ray casting. It distinguishes itself by using explicit geometric primitives (disks) rather than implicit distance fields, allowing for direct analytical occlusion calculation between primitives.
+Approximates surface geometry as a collection of disk-shaped elements at mesh vertices to compute AO and bent normals analytically, avoiding the cost of ray casting. It distinguishes itself by using explicit geometric primitives (disks) rather than implicit distance fields, allowing for direct analytical occlusion calculation between primitives. Choose this for static meshes where analytical bent normals are needed.
 
 ### [Monte Carlo Ambient Occlusion with Distance Mapping](4335-monte-carlo-ambient-occlusion-with-distance-mapping.md)
-Estimates AO by casting rays in random directions and numerically integrating the occlusion contribution using a distance mapping function. It differs from deterministic grid-based or screen-space methods by relying on stochastic sampling, allowing for flexible handling of complex geometry at the cost of noise and higher sample counts for convergence.
+Estimates AO by casting rays in random directions and numerically integrating the occlusion contribution using a distance mapping function. It differs from deterministic grid-based or screen-space methods by relying on stochastic sampling, allowing for flexible handling of complex geometry at the cost of noise and higher sample counts for convergence. Choose this when integrating AO into a general Monte Carlo path tracer.
 
